\title{North Mesopotamian Arabic Language Resources for K-12 Curriculum Development}
\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
North Mesopotamian Arabic (ISO 639-3 ayp), also called Moslawi or Mardelli, is a variety of Mesopotamian Arabic spoken in Iraq, Syria and Turkey. It belongs to the \emph{qeltu} group of dialects, as distinguished from the \emph{gelet} group. This survey found mainly scholarly descriptions; no school materials in the dialect were found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes in North Mesopotamian Arabic were found in the searches. Schooling in the region uses Standard Arabic (or Turkish in Turkey).

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item G. Grigore, \emph{L'arabe parl\'e \`a Mardin: monographie d'un parler arabe p\'eriph\'erique} (French): phonetics, morphology, numerals and other parts of speech of the Mardin dialect. \url{https://gorgiaspress.com/larabe-parle-a-mardin-monographie-dun-parler-arabe-peripherique}
\item Review/article on the Mardin Arabic monograph (Romanian Review of Arabic studies, University of Bucharest). \url{https://journals.unibuc.ro/index.php/roar/en/article/view/1894/2011}
\item Wikipedia overview of North Mesopotamian Arabic. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Mesopotamian_Arabic}
\item ASJP word-list entry for North Mesopotamian Arabic. \url{https://asjp.clld.org/languages/NORTH_MESOPOTAMIAN_ARABIC}
\end{itemize}
No dedicated dictionary of the dialect was found.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found in the searches.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Arabic-language article on the Mardin Arabic dialect (Diwan al-Arab). \url{https://diwanalarab.com/%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%87%D8%AC%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%AF%D9%84%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A9.html}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Materials are scholarly and mostly in French, German and Romanian. A curriculum would need to be built from dialect descriptions and new community-based material, alongside Standard Arabic.

\end{document}
