\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Ayi (ISO 639-3 ayq; Glottolog ayip1235) is a Sepik (Tama) language of southeastern Sandaun Province, Papua New Guinea, spoken in three villages between the Sepik River and Nuku. Speaker counts differ: 430 in the 2000 census, 590 per Global Recordings Network. Alternate names include Besi and Warasai. Very little material exists.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes were found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia entry on the Ayi language. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayi_language}
\item Glottolog entry for Ayi. \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/ayip1235}
\end{itemize}
No grammar or dictionary was found in the searches.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile of the Ayi people. \url{https://joshuaproject.net/index.php/people_groups/14328}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Global Recordings Network listing for Ayi. \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/ayq}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry. \url{https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/10698?hl=fr}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Documentation is minimal. Any curriculum would require new fieldwork with the community; the sources found are catalogue entries only.

\end{document}
